      "textEn": "131\n\nSreesukha said,\nHaving touched the water, he was bitten by the tiger. Nay mam dyumatah parshvam viirasyaeti aha sarathim. 1>. Meaning of the word.\n\nShri-Shuka said—Shukadeva Goswami said; Sah-He (Pradyumna); Upasprushya—Having touched; Salilam—Water; Danshitah—Having bitten; Kavach—Wearing the armor; Dhrut—Having held; Karmukh—His bow; Nay—Having taken; Mam—To me; Dyumat-His arrow; Parshvam—On the side.\nPascha—Near; Veersya—Of the brave; Iti—Thus; Aah—Said; Sarathim—To his charioteer. Shukdev Goswami said: After refreshing oneself with water, wearing one's armor,\nand taking his bow, Lord Pradyumna said to his charioteer, \"Take me back to where the brave Dyuman is standing.\"\n\"Come, let's go to where the brave Dyuman is standing.\"\"\n\nMeaning: Pradyumna wanted to correct this mistake that when he fainted, his charioteer\nhad brought him away from the battlefield. The son of Rukmini, destroying his own army.\nPratikrasta pratyavighnya narachairashtabhi smayan. 2. Meaning of the word:\nVidhamantam—by making a laughing sound; Swa—own; Sainyan—to the soldiers; Dyumantham—to the demons; Rukmini-suta—son of Rukmini;\n( Pradyumna ); Pratihatya—by counterattacking; Pratyavidhyat—by counterattacking; Narachaih—with special iron-made arrows;\nAshtabhih—eight; Smayan—laughing. In the absence of Pradyumna, Dyumna was destroying his army, but now\nPradyumna attacked Dyumna in return and, laughing, pierced him with eight arrows. Meaning: Srila Vishwanath Chakravarti's commentary is that Pradyumna challenged Dyuman by saying,\n“Now, try killing me!” Saying this, and giving Dyuman the opportunity to wield the weapon, Pradyumna\nshot his deadly arrows. Chaturbhischaturaso vahanasutamekan chaahanat. Dvabhyam dhanushcha ketum cha sharrenanyena vai shirah. 3. Lexicon"
