      "textEn": "12\n\nThoughts. But only by serving the lotus feet of Lord Krishna, the devotees are freed from the ego of the body.\nThey go. Then how can such a delusion affect God at any time? Those who strike with weapons, with a smile,\nShalva with arrows, Shauriramo Ghaghvikrama. Having drawn the bow, the crest, the arrow,\nThe auspiciousness, the enemy's compassion, the joy. 33. Meaning of the word\n\nTam—that; Shastra—of weapons; Pugaiha—with a flurry; Praharanam—attacking; Ojasah—with great force; Shalvam—of Shalva; Sharahi—Krishna; Amoghah—incorrect; Vikramah—his prowess; Viddhva—by piercing; Achhinat—he broke; Vardham—camel; Dhanu—bow; Shirah—of the head; Manim—of the gem; Saubham—of the chariot; Cha—and; Shatroohah—of the enemy; Gadaya—with his mace; Ruroj—broken; Ha—indeed. When Shalya was fiercely attacking them with a barrage of weapons, then the infallible mighty one,\nLord Krishna released his arrows on Shalya, which caused him to be wounded, and his armor,\nbow, and the gem of the crown were destroyed.\nThen he, with his mace, tore apart his enemy's chariot. Meaning: Srila Prabhupada writes, “When Shalva thought that Krishna was fascinated by his yogic performance,\nhe became encouraged and with more power and strength, began to shower numerous arrows on the Lord. But the enthusiasm of Shalva can be compared to kites falling rapidly in the fire.\nCan be compared. Lord Krishna, with immense power, wounded Shalva by throwing an arrow,\ndue to which his armor, bow, and jewel-studded crown were shattered into pieces. From the blow of Krishna's mace,\nShalva's wonderful plane was shattered into pieces and fell into the sea.”\n\nShalva's negligible yogic power could not enchant Krishna. This fact is emphasized here.\nIt is shown while emphasizing. That which is scattered by the hands of Krishna,\nfalls into the fire with a thousand streams. Having abandoned that earth, he stood there, holding the mace.\nMoving, he shrugged, and the unyielding one approached. 3."
