      "textEn": "13\n\nMeaning of the word\n\nTat—that (Saubha); Krishna-hasta—from Krishna's hand; Eritaya—turned; Vichurnitam—churned; Papta—fell;\nToye—in the water; Gadaya—with the mace; Sahasradha—in thousands; Visrijya—left; Tat—this; Bhu-talam—on the earth;\nAasthitah—standing; Gadam—his mace; Udyamya—taking; Shalva—Shalva; Achyutam—on Lord Krishna; Abhyagat—entered;\nAakramita—attacked; Drutam—quickly. The Sauvabha Viman, shattered into thousands of pieces by Lord Krishna's mace, fell into the ocean. Shalva left it and stood on the ground. He picked up his mace and ran towards Lord\nAchyuta. Having approached, he severed his arm with a thunderbolt.\nHaving cut it off, he wondrously placed the chariot's axle.\nThe slain one, with a slender body, like a serpent,\nBorn of the serpent, like a serpent, the sun's rising. 35. Meaning of the word:\n\nAadhavatah—Running towards him; Sa-gadam—With his mace; Tasya—His; Bahum—The arms; Bhallena—With a special type of arrow; Chhitva—Cutting; Ath—Then; Ratha-angam—With his wheel; Adbhutam—Wonderful; Vadhaya—To kill; Shalvasya—Of Shalva; Laya—At the time of destruction; Ark—The sun; Sanibhram—Exactly; Bibhrat—Having caught; Bavah—Started shining; Sa-Arkah—Along with the sun; Iva—As if; Udayah—Of sunrise; Achalah—A mountain. When Shalva lunged towards him, God released a spear and cut off that arm\nof his in which he was holding the mace. In the end, deciding to kill Shalva, Lord\nKrishna raised his Sudarshan Chakra, which looked like the sun appearing at the time of the cosmic destruction. Shining brightly, the Lord appeared as if from Udayachal, who holds the rising sun.\nIt holds. Whose head is adorned with a crown,\nAnd who is adorned with a crest, the supreme Purusha, Hari.\nLike a thunderbolt to Vritra,\nHe became a destroyer of the pride of the gods. 36. Meaning of the word\n\nJahar—cut off; Ten—from this; Ev—indeed; Shirah—head; Sa—with; Kundalam—earring; Kirit—crown;\nYuktam—worn; Puru—extended; Mayinah—endowed wit